# Common pitfalls (Unity style guide)

From [Unity's C# style guide](https://unity.com/resources/c-sharp-style-guide-unity-6).

Clean code is **deliberate teamwork**, not accident. Unclean code still happens — stay on the hunt. A **code smell** is a warning sign, not always a bug. Investigate when you see these:

| Smell | What to check | See |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **Enigmatic naming** | Names should state intent without a comment | `unity-csharp-naming` |
| **Needless complexity** | God types, long methods, over-anticipating future needs | Split into focused classes — SRP in `unity-clean-code-principles` |
| **Inflexibility** | One small change forces edits everywhere | Often multiple responsibilities on one type — SRP |
| **Fragility** | Tiny change breaks unrelated systems | Hidden coupling; missing tests; shared mutable state |
| **Immobility** | Code cannot be reused without dragging many dependencies | Inject or narrow interfaces; decouple from scene singletons |
| **Duplicate code** | Copy-paste blocks diverge over time | Extract once, call many — DRY in `unity-clean-code-principles` |
| **Excessive commentary** | Line-by-line narration of obvious code | Rename/refactor/split; comment **why** only — `unity-csharp-comments` |

Smells overlap (a God class is often fragile *and* inflexible). Fix the underlying design, not the symptom alone.

## Unity-specific traps

Worth extra scrutiny in this project:

| Trap | Prefer |
|------|--------|
| `Find*` / `GetComponent` every frame | Resolve once; cache references |
| Listeners, coroutines, tweens after disable | Clean up in `OnDisable` / `OnDestroy` |
| `public` fields for Inspector only | `[SerializeField]` + `m_` fields |
| Shared `ScriptableObject` mutated at runtime | Instances or explicit runtime data objects |
| `tag == "Player"` | `CompareTag("Player")` |
| Editor APIs in player builds | `#if UNITY_EDITOR` or Editor assemblies |
| UI: per-frame `Q()`, inline styles | Cached queries; BEM in USS — `unity-ui-toolkit` |
| New/touched `.cs` missing `using` | Mirror sibling file imports; Unity recompile — **Handoff — `using` directives** below |
| New/touched `.cs` duplicate `using` | Scan top-of-file `using` block; remove identical lines — **Handoff — `using` directives** below |

When touching legacy code, address smells in the area you are already changing — avoid unplanned repo-wide cleanups.

## Handoff — `using` directives (missing and duplicate)

### Missing `using` on new type references

**Symptom:** Unity compile **CS0246** (`type or namespace name 'X' could not be found`) or **CS1503** (cannot convert `X?` to `GridDungeon....X?`) after adding a file or referencing types from another namespace or assembly.

**Cause:** New `.cs` copied patterns from a sibling but **omitted `using`** for namespaces imported elsewhere. Cross-asmdef types need both an **asmdef reference** and a **`using`**. IDE may not flag until full Unity recompile; an unqualified name can resolve to the wrong type → CS1503 on the next line.

**Fix — before handoff or commit** (every **new or touched** `.cs` in the change set):

1. List **unqualified type names** in the file; add **`using`** at the top for each namespace (or fully qualify only when two namespaces collide — `unity-csharp-naming.mdc`).
2. **Mirror imports** from the nearest sibling in the same folder (e.g. `*Builder` ↔ `*Projector`, fixture ↔ test).
3. **Cross-assembly:** confirm `.asmdef` references (`unity-core-campaign-assembly.mdc`) **and** namespace imports — Core vs Campaign vs Runtime vs Tests each have distinct roots.
4. Run `Tools/request-compile-status.ps1` when the Editor may be open, or `Tools/read-unity-editor-log.ps1` as fallback — do not assume green IDE squiggles alone. See `unity-compile-status-agent.mdc`.

**Example ([#315](https://github.com/miramocha/griddungeon-game/issues/315)):** `SynchroBarPresentationProjector` used `CampaignSaveData` without `using GridDungeon.Core.SaveData;`.

### Duplicate `using` lines

**Symptom:** Same `using Some.Namespace;` appears twice (or more) at the top of a file — common when merging imports from a sibling or pasting a block twice. Compiles; review and IDE cleanup often miss it until a human or reviewer spots it.

**Cause:** Agent or manual edit appended `using` lines without scanning the existing import block.

**Fix — before handoff or commit** (every **new or touched** `.cs` in the change set):

1. Read the **`using` block** at the top of each changed file (stop at first `namespace` or type declaration).
2. Remove **identical** `using` lines — same namespace, including `using static` and `using Alias = ...` (must match exactly).
3. Keep one copy; preserve sensible order (System → third-party → project namespaces) after removal.
4. Optional quick scan on changed paths (PowerShell, game repo root):

```powershell
$paths = @(
  (git diff --name-only HEAD -- '*.cs'),
  (git diff --name-only -- '*.cs'),
  (git ls-files --others --exclude-standard -- '*.cs')
) | Sort-Object -Unique
foreach ($path in $paths) {
  if (-not (Test-Path $path)) { continue }
  $seen = @{}
  Select-String -Path $path -Pattern '^\s*using\s+(.+);' | ForEach-Object {
    $ns = $_.Matches[0].Groups[1].Value.Trim()
    if ($seen.ContainsKey($ns)) { Write-Host "DUPLICATE using '$ns' in $path" }
    else { $seen[$ns] = $true }
  }
}
```

**Agents:** run missing- and duplicate-`using` checks in `format-before-handoff-and-commit.mdc` procedure when changed `.cs` exists (after CSharpier, before posting test plan or committing).
